  to [38;5;153m<script type="module" src="app.js">[39m; the storage backend is passed in, so it can be memory, [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m or
  [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m, and tests can substitute a fake. [3mWins when[0m you expect several more modules soon and want real boundaries
  and testability from the start. [3mCosts:[0m module scripts are deferred and don't create globals, so [38;5;153mlogin[39m and
  [38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m stop being reachable from the console or inline handlers — a real behavior change for a 28-line app, in
  service of structure that doesn't exist yet. Complexity: moderate.

  [1mC. No client-side store — re-read the id from the server[0m — persist nothing. Any page that needs the id asks a
  [38;5;153m/me[39m-style endpoint; the server session is already the source of truth. [3mWins when[0m correctness matters more than
  latency: nothing can go stale, nothing to clear on logout, no user-editable copy. [3mCosts:[0m a round trip per page, and it
  needs a server endpoint that does not exist — [38;5;153mlogin[39m is a stub that never calls [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m. Complexity: moderate,
  and most of it isn't on our side.

  [1mMy recommendation: A.[0m It's the only one whose cost is proportional to what you asked for. B buys structure for
  components that are still hypothetical and pays for it by breaking the global-script model the page currently relies
  on; if the app really does grow, moving A's module to ES modules later is a mechanical change because the interface
  ([38;5;153mset[39m/[38;5;153mget[39m/[38;5;153mclear[39m) stays identical. C is the architecturally cleanest answer and I'd revisit it the moment [38;5;153mlogin[39m becomes
  a real network call — but today it would be building a client for an endpoint nobody has written.

  One sub-decision inside A that I'd rather you make than assume: [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m (cleared when the tab closes) versus
  [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m (survives browser restart until explicitly cleared). For a display-only id I'd default to [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m
  — a stale "logged in as…" banner after a browser restart is a worse failure than asking someone to log in again — but
  [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m is the right call if you want the id to outlive the tab.
